== '''Rectangular Tombs (長方形墳, Chōhōkeifun)''' ==
A ''rectangular tomb'' (''chōhōkeifun'') is a type of Kofun (kofun) constructed during the Kofun period, characterized by a rectangular-shaped Kofun. These tombs are found across Japan and exhibit a variety of sizes and structures depending on the region and time period.

== Characteristics ==
As the name suggests, rectangular tombs have a rectangular-shaped mound when viewed from above. The size of these mounds varies, ranging from small-scale to large-scale constructions. Some tombs feature surrounding moats (''shūgō'', 周濠) or embankments (''shūtei'', 周堤), and burial facilities within them may include ''tateana-shiki sekishitsu'' (pit-style stone chambers) or ''yokoana-shiki sekishitsu'' (horizontal stone chambers).

== Distribution and Period ==
Rectangular tombs were primarily built from the mid to late Kofun period. They are most commonly found in regions such as the Kinki (Kansai) and Chūgoku areas but have also been identified in other parts of Japan. The distribution and construction periods of these tombs are thought to reflect the political and social structures of the time, as well as interregional interactions.

== Significance ==
The construction of rectangular tombs highlights the diversity of burial practices among rulers and influential figures of the time. These tombs serve as important archaeological resources for understanding regional cultural characteristics and burial customs. Additionally, comparative studies with other tomb types help shed light on the social structure and power dynamics of the Kofun period.

== History ==
In the latter half to the end of the sixth century, as the keyhole-shaped tumuli [前方後円墳] that had previously served as chieftain tombs [首長墓] went into decline and disappeared, a new type of final-stage square tumulus [方墳] emerged. Unlike earlier square tumuli [方墳], these final-stage square tumuli [方墳] appeared as a new burial form for sovereigns [大王墓] or chieftains [首長墓].<ref name=":0">https://adeac.jp/yukuhashi-city/text-list/d100010/ht1024406020</ref>

The Kasuga Mukoyama Kofun in Osaka Prefecture (traditionally attributed to Emperor Yōmei [伝用明天皇陵]), dated to the end of the sixth century through the early seventh century, measures 65 meters east-to-west and 60 meters north-to-south. Similarly, the Akasaka Tennōzan Kofun in Nara Prefecture (traditionally attributed to Emperor Sushun [伝崇峻天皇陵]) measures 45.5 meters east-to-west and 42.2 meters north-to-south. The Yamada Takatsuka Kofun in Osaka Prefecture (traditionally attributed to Empress Suiko [伝推古天皇陵]), from around the first half of the seventh century, measures 59 meters east-to-west and 55 meters north-to-south. All of these are rectangular tumuli [長方形墳].<ref name=":0" />

The Ishibutai Kofun in Nara Prefecture, said to be the tomb of Soga no Umako from around the first half of the seventh century, is a square tumulus [方墳] measuring 51 meters on each side. Likewise, the Iwayayama Kofun from the middle of the seventh century is said to be a round-top, square-bottom tumulus [上円下方墳] with each side measuring 45 meters.<ref name=":0" />

From the end of the sixth century through the first half of the seventh century, it appears that slightly elongated rectangular tumuli [長方形墳] were standardized as replacements for keyhole-shaped tumuli [前方後円墳] for use as sovereigns’ tombs [大王墓] or chieftains’ tombs [首長墓].<ref name=":0" />
